-- title:
--   Example 3.1.7 — the binomial market is free of arbitrage iff $d<1+i<u$
-- statement:
--   Consider the binomial (Cox–Ross–Rubinstein) market with $N\ge1$ periods: one bond with constant interest rate $i_n\equiv i$ and one stock whose relative price changes $\tilde R_1,\dots,\tilde R_N$ are independent and identically distributed with
--   $$
--   \mathbb P(\tilde R_n=u)=p,\qquad \mathbb P(\tilde R_n=d)=1-p,\qquad 0<d<u,\ 0<p<1,
--   $$
--   and filtration $\mathcal F_n=\sigma(S_0,\dots,S_n)$. Then the market is free of arbitrage if and only if
--   $$
--   d<1+i<u .
--   $$
--   The binomial market is the standard example of the book's portfolio problems; this is the condition under which they are well posed.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: the $N$-period market of §3.1 on $(\Omega,\mathcal F,(\mathcal F_n),\mathbb P)$ with $\mathbb R^d$, time $n=0,\dots,N$ and $x\cdot y=\sum_k x_ky_k$ → `FinancialMarket`: data indexed by $n\in\mathbb N$ and constrained only for the times the book uses, $\mathbb R^d$ as `Fin d → ℝ`, inner products as finite sums; reason: the values at unused times never enter a statement; same objects
--   - reading: deterministic initial prices $s^k_0$, adapted prices $S^k_n$, $\tilde R^k_{n+1}>0$ a.s. → in addition $s^k_0>0$ and each $\tilde R_n$ is $\mathcal F$-measurable; reason: p.61 calls $\tilde R_n$ random variables on $(\Omega,\mathcal F,\mathbb P)$ and says the stock price stays positive, so these are the book's own standing assumptions made explicit
--   - reading: up and down probabilities $p$ and $1-p$ ("can take two values", p.60) → $0<p<1$; reason: for $p=1$ and $d<1+i<u$ the stock beats the bond surely and buying it is an arbitrage, so the example presupposes that both values occur
--   - reading: "the binomial financial market" (filtration not named) → $\mathcal F_n=\sigma(S_0,\dots,S_n)$, $N\ge1$; reason: the book's argument uses deterministic $\varphi$, i.e. Remark 3.1.6, whose setting is $\mathcal F^S$; with a filtration revealing $\tilde R_{n+1}$ at time $n$ there is an arbitrage although $d<1+i<u$, and for $N=0$ nothing is traded
--   - encoding: i.i.d. $\tilde R_n$ with $\mathbb P(\tilde R_n=u)=p$, $\mathbb P(\tilde R_n=d)=1-p$ → the two probabilities for each $n\le N$ and `iIndepFun` of $(\tilde R_1,\dots,\tilde R_N)$; the down factor is named `dn`; reason: the two probabilities fix the common law, so identical distribution follows
-- source:
--   Bäuerle & Rieder, Markov Decision Processes with Applications to Finance (2011), Example 3.1.7, p.65 (PDF 79); binomial model, p.60 (PDF 74)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_NoArbitrage_FinancialMarket_price
import Definitions.Def_BauerleRiederMDP_NoArbitrage_FinancialMarket_ArbitrageFree

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace BauerleRiederMDP.NoArbitrage

/-- Example 3.1.7 (Bäuerle & Rieder, Markov Decision Processes with Applications to Finance
(2011), Example 3.1.7, p.65, PDF 79, with the model of p.60, PDF 74): in the binomial
(Cox–Ross–Rubinstein) financial market a necessary and sufficient condition for the absence of
arbitrage opportunities is `d < 1 + i < u`.

The binomial market has one bond with constant interest rate `i_n ≡ i` and one stock whose
relative price changes `R̃_1, …, R̃_N` are independent and identically distributed with
`ℙ(R̃_n = u) = p`, `ℙ(R̃_n = d) = 1 − p`, where `0 < d < u`.

**Formalization Note** The market has `d = 1` risky asset (`Fin 1`); the down factor is written
`dn`. The probability `p` lies in `(0, 1)`, so that both moves occur (for `p ∈ {0, 1}` the claim is
false). The filtration is the one generated by the prices, `𝓕_n = σ(S_0, …, S_n)`, the setting of
Remark 3.1.6 on which the book's argument rests. At least one period is traded (`1 ≤ N`). -/
theorem example_3_1_7 {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] {P : Measure Ω} [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (M : FinancialMarket Ω P 1) (i u dn p : ℝ) (hN : 1 ≤ M.N)
    (hi : ∀ n, 1 ≤ n → n ≤ M.N → M.i n = i)
    (hdn : 0 < dn) (hdu : dn < u) (hp0 : 0 < p) (hp1 : p < 1)
    (hup : ∀ n, 1 ≤ n → n ≤ M.N → P {ω | M.Rt n ω 0 = u} = ENNReal.ofReal p)
    (hdown : ∀ n, 1 ≤ n → n ≤ M.N → P {ω | M.Rt n ω 0 = dn} = ENNReal.ofReal (1 - p))
    (hindep : iIndepFun (fun k : Fin M.N => M.Rt ((k : ℕ) + 1)) P)
    (hF : ∀ n, n ≤ M.N →
      M.F n = ⨆ t ∈ Finset.range (n + 1), MeasurableSpace.comap (M.price t) inferInstance) :
    M.ArbitrageFree ↔ dn < 1 + i ∧ 1 + i < u := by sorry

end BauerleRiederMDP.NoArbitrage
